What:		MADV_WILLNEED on shmem with failed swapin entries
Date:		November 2026
Contact:	Linux memory management mailing list <linux-mm@kvack.org>
Description:
		MADV_WILLNEED reports EIO if its shared-memory range contains
		an existing poisoned swapin entry. It still submits read-ahead
		for other entries in the range. It does not wait for newly
		submitted I/O, and success does not guarantee completion.

		A caller that needs to detect asynchronous read failure without
		accessing its mapping can read the backing file with pread().
		Shmem returns EIO on failed swapin, replaces the entry with a
		poison marker, and releases its swap and swap-cache references.
		A mapped access continues to report SIGBUS; missing remote data
		is never replaced with a readable zero page.

		An application that owns disposable cache contents can remove
		the failed range with FALLOC_FL_PUNCH_HOLE | FALLOC_FL_KEEP_SIZE
		after fencing all users of it. This removes backing pages and
		poison markers from every shared mapping. New writes allocate
		new backing pages. Anonymous swap faults retain their existing
		SIGBUS behavior and are not covered by this recovery contract.
